WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.551
Setting up a Mac for local inference starts with a stable base system.

00:00:04.551 --> 00:00:12.678
macOS version, command line tools, package managers, Python environments, runtime installation, and model storage all matter.

00:00:12.678 --> 00:00:24.966
The exact toolchain depends on the runtime, but the operating habit is consistent: make installation steps repeatable and avoid turning the daily-use system into an undocumented experiment.

00:00:24.966 --> 00:00:34.328
Package managers and Python environments are useful, but they can also create confusion when dependencies are installed globally without a plan.

00:00:34.328 --> 00:00:38.294
Keep runtime-specific environments separated where practical.

00:00:38.294 --> 00:00:46.746
Document which tools are installed, where model files live, which commands start services, and which ports or interfaces are used.

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:50.257
Small notes save large troubleshooting sessions later.

00:00:50.257 --> 00:01:01.894
Containers may be useful at a high level for packaging some application dependencies, but Apple Silicon container workflows have their own platform and performance considerations.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:08.916
Containers do not remove the need to understand storage, permissions, networking, and runtime compatibility.

00:01:08.916 --> 00:01:17.108
For many local LLM users, a native runtime plus clean documentation is simpler than forcing every experiment into a container.

00:01:17.108 --> 00:01:19.383
Update discipline is part of setup.

00:01:19.383 --> 00:01:25.365
A macOS update, package update, runtime update, or model manager update can change behavior.

00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:31.931
Before changing a stable system, record the current working state and keep a practical rollback path.

00:01:31.931 --> 00:01:41.618
The goal is to let experiments change while the base system remains understandable, recoverable, and safe to use with sensitive prompts or documents.
